On Tue, Jul 10, 2012 at 6:52 AM, .arpis. <rpglover64+jbobau@gmail.com>wrote:

> I'm correcting you; {cmene} refer to names, e.g. {zo .arpis. cmene mi .i
> ji'a zo cipni cmene la'oi Paul}. They refer to the use/meaning of a word;
> they are also, more or less, language angostic. {cmevla} refers to a
> morphological construct, and is primarily useful in lojban; they must end
> in a consonant (and if you aren't on the dot side, can't contain "la",
> "lai", or "doi" at the start of a syllable). They must always be followed
> by a pause, and, like all lojban words, if they start with a vowel, they
> must be preceded by a pause.
>
> Elaborating on the rest of the discussion:
> Under current convention (dot side), whenever a cmevla is used in a cmene,
> (e.g. {la .mlat.}) it must be surrounded by pauses in speech, which some
> people take pains to indicate with the dot character. Pauses in names have
> no meaning (besides word separation) when brivla are used in them.
>
